# Normalize text to LF in the working tree on every platform. Without this,
# Windows checks out CRLF via core.autocrlf and `prettier --check` fails on every
# JavaScript file at once -- 37 of them, on the first run of the format gate --
# because .prettierrc.json pins endOfLine to lf.
#
# The binary rules below re-clear `text` for their own patterns; the last matching
# line wins per attribute, so a binary keeps `-text` and is never munged.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Nothing here is in LFS. The `-text` is the whole job: it stops the rule above
# rewriting line endings inside a binary, which is the one way git corrupts an
# image without anything reporting an error.
#
# LFS was removed rather than kept for the large files, and the reason is that
# it never fit what this repository stores. Its costs are metered -- storage and
# bandwidth, both cumulative -- and there is no eviction of any kind, so every
# version ever pushed is charged for permanently and no command reclaims it. An
# earlier bandwidth overrun here was traced to eleven CI checkouts pulling a
# 23.8 MB payload that no job read, and the fix removed the last workflow that
# touched LFS at all. What remained was a metered store nothing automated used.
#
# A plain blob has no quota and no bill. What it does have is permanence: an
# image committed here is in the history for good and every clone fetches it, so
# the thing to control is churn rather than the storage mechanism. Regenerated
# output is worth committing when the picture changes and not when only its
# bytes do -- an encoder that emits a different file for an identical scene is
# spending history on nothing.
*.png -text
*.jpg -text
*.jpeg -text
*.webp -text
*.pdf -text
*.bmp -text
*.gif -text
*.apng -text
*.avif -text
*.ico -text
*.ttf -text
*.otf -text
*.woff -text
*.woff2 -text

# Test fixtures keep their own line because the rules above no longer say
# anything about filters. `-filter -diff -merge` is what guarantees they are
# never handed to a clean/smudge filter, whatever is configured globally.
#
# Cargo.toml's `include` ships tests/assets/images/pentagon.png and the Oswald
# variable font inside the crate, and they package as real bytes from any
# checkout.
#
# `text` is left alone here: the per-extension rules above already clear it for
# every binary form, and the licence and SVG files under this path want the
# repository-wide LF normalisation that the first rule gives them.
tests/assets/** -filter -diff -merge
